# Haryana Personalities and Literature

> Haryana has produced chief ministers who shaped national politics, a
> farmers' leader knighted by the British, an astronaut and a folk-theatre
> tradition that still fills village squares. Exams such as HSSC CET and
> Haryana Police ask "who was the first CM", "where was X born" and "who is
> the saang master of Y" almost every cycle. This lesson fixes each
> person to a birth place, a date and one anchor fact.

## 1. Chief Ministers Who Made Haryana

Haryana became a state on 1 November 1966. The first six CMs in order of
appearance are easy to jumble, so learn the anchor facts.

| Leader | Native place | Term(s) and anchor fact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Bhagwat Dayal Sharma | Beri, Jhajjar (b. 1918, d. 1993) | First CM, 1 Nov 1966 to March 1967; later Governor of Odisha and of Madhya Pradesh |
| Rao Birender Singh | Rewari (b. 1921, d. 2009) | CM 24 March to 20 Nov 1967; formed the Vishal Haryana Party after leaving Congress |
| Bansi Lal | Golagarh, Bhiwani (b. 1927, d. 2006) | Three terms: 1968-75, 1986-87, 1996-99; called the "architect of modern Haryana" |
| Devi Lal | Teja Khera, Sirsa (b. 1914, d. 2001) | CM 1977-79 and 1987-89; nickname "Tau"; Deputy Prime Minister 1989-91 |
| Bhajan Lal | Koranwali (now in Pakistan); long-time MLA from Adampur, Hisar (b. 1930, d. 2011) | CM 1979 to mid-1980s and again 1991-96 |

[definition]
**Aya Ram Gaya Ram** is the phrase born of the 1967 Haryana politics, when
legislators changed parties repeatedly within days. It is the origin of
the word "defection" in Indian political talk and led, in time, to the
anti-defection law.
[/definition]

Other facts to pin down:

- Bansi Lal also served as Union Defence Minister (1975-77) and Railway Minister (1984-86). He founded the **Haryana Vikas Party** in 1996. The Bansi Lal canal (earlier Jui canal) and Chaudhary Bansi Lal University, Bhiwani (2014) carry his name.
- Devi Lal founded the **Indian National Lok Dal** in 1996 ; his memorial in Delhi is called **Sangharsh Sthal**.
- Bhajan Lal moved with his whole Janata ministry into Congress, a famous example of defection. He later served as Union Minister of Environment and Forests and of Agriculture, and founded the Haryana Janhit Congress in 2007.
- The **Pandit Bhagwat Dayal Sharma University of Health Sciences** in Rohtak is named after the first CM.

## 2. Chhotu Ram and the Freedom Struggle

**Sir Chhotu Ram** (born 24 November 1881 at Garhi Sampla, Rohtak; died 9
January 1945 at Lahore) is the best-known farmers' leader of undivided
Punjab. He founded the weekly **Jat Gazette** in Rohtak in 1916, co-founded
the Zamindara (later Unionist) Party and was Revenue Minister of Punjab
from 1937 until his death. He was knighted in 1937. He is remembered as
**Deenbandhu** ("friend of the poor") and **Rehbar-e-Azam**.

| Law or measure | Aim |
| --- | --- |
| Punjab Relief of Indebtedness Act, 1934 | Debt conciliation boards and limits on interest |
| Punjab Debtors' Protection Act, 1936 | Shielded debtors from arrest and attachment |
| Punjab Restitution of Mortgaged Lands Act, 1938 | Farmers could reclaim mortgaged land by repaying the principal |
| Market regulation law (Mandi Samiti), 1940 | Regulated markets for farmers' produce |

Other freedom-struggle names:

- **Rao Tula Ram** (born about 1825, Rampura near Rewari). He took Rewari in May 1857 with his cousin Rao Gopal Dev. His forces fought the **Battle of Narnaul (Nasibpur) on 16 November 1857**, led by his cousin Rao Krishan Singh. He left India to seek foreign help and died at **Kabul** on 23 September 1863. He is the hero of the Ahirwal region.
- The 1857 uprising in Haryana began with the revolt at **Ambala** cantonment and spread across the region.

## 3. Kalpana Chawla: from Karnal to Space

[example]
Kalpana Chawla was born in Karnal on 17 March 1962 (some sources give
1 July 1961). She studied at Tagore Baal Niketan school, Karnal, and
earned a Bachelor of Engineering from Punjab Engineering College in 1982.
Her first flight was on **STS-87** from 19 November 1997, making her the
first woman of Indian origin in space. She died on **1 February 2003**
when Space Shuttle Columbia broke up on re-entry on mission **STS-107**
(launched 16 January 2003). India's weather satellite MetSat-1 was renamed
**Kalpana-1** in her honour.
[/example]

Exam trap: Kalpana Chawla was an American citizen of Indian origin, and the
disaster happened on re-entry, not at launch.

## 4. Saang, Ragini and the Folk Masters

**Saang** (also called swang) is Haryana's folk musical theatre, performed
in open spaces with a lead actor-singer, a chorus and drums, usually
telling a legendary or moral tale. A **ragini** is a folk song form closely
tied to saang; the ragini style was popularised by Pandit Lakhmi Chand.

| Master | Birth place | Anchor fact |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Pandit Lakhmi Chand | Janti Kalan, Sonipat (1903-1945) | Called "Suryakavi" and "Kalidas of Haryana"; saangs include Heer Ranjha, Meera Bai and Hiramal Jamal |
| Pandit Mange Ram | Susana | One of Lakhmi Chand's best-known disciples |
| Baje Bhagat | Sisana, Sonipat (1898-1939) | Saang artist and ragini poet; early 1920s fame |
| Dayachand Mayna | Mayna, Rohtak (1915-1993) | About 21 kissas and over 150 raginis; many disciples |

The Haryana Sahitya Akademi's honours reflect these names: awards named
after **Pt. Lakhmi Chand** and **Dayachand Mayna** exist alongside others.

## 5. Haryana Sahitya Akademi and State Honours

The **Haryana Sahitya Akademi** (headquartered at Panchkula) promotes Hindi and
Haryanvi literature through annual awards, manuscript grants and
publication support.

| Award | Notes |
| --- | --- |
| Mahakavi Surdas Ajeevan Sahitya Sadhna Samman | A lifetime-achievement honour (Rs 5 lakh when Dr Sant Ram Deswal received it in 2018) |
| Pt. Madhav Prasad Mishra Samman | Named after a Hindi writer |
| Babu Balmukund Gupta Samman | Named after the Rewari journalist and critic |
| Lala Deshbandhu Gupta Samman | Named after a freedom fighter and editor |
| Pt. Lakhmi Chand Samman | Folk-literature honour |
| Janakavi Mehr Singh Samman | Named after the folk poet Mehr Singh |
| Haryana Gaurav Samman | One of the Akademi's annual honours, listed with the awards above |

Award amounts are revised from time to time, so treat money figures as
dated facts. Dr **Sant Ram Deswal** (born 1955, Khedka, Jhajjar) won the
Mahakavi Surdas honour in 2018 and a Padma Shri in 2025 for literature
and education.

[summary]
First CM: Bhagwat Dayal Sharma (1966-67); second: Rao Birender Singh (1967,
Vishal Haryana Party). Bansi Lal: Bhiwani, three terms, modern Haryana.
Devi Lal: Sirsa, Tau, Deputy PM, INLD. Bhajan Lal: 1979 and 1991 terms.
Chhotu Ram: Garhi Sampla, Jat Gazette 1916, Deenbandhu, Rehbar-e-Azam,
Revenue Minister 1937. Rao Tula Ram: Rewari, Narnaul battle 16 Nov 1857,
died Kabul. Kalpana Chawla: Karnal, STS-87 (1997), STS-107 (died 1 Feb
2003). Saang masters: Lakhmi Chand (Janti Kalan), Mange Ram (Susana),
Baje Bhagat (Sisana), Dayachand Mayna. Sahitya Akademi: Panchkula.
[/summary]
